<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Martos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Martos is a town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaen">Jaén</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, below the rock known as the Peña de Martos; it was the Roman colony of Tucci. Its castle was handed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> by al-Bayyasi in the 1220s and then given to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-calatrava">Order of Calatrava</a>, which held it as a frontier stronghold facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>. The town is now a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> production.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/martos.jpg" alt="Martos" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Xapis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pe%C3%B1a_de_Martos.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Martos is a city and municipality in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaen">Jaén</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>. The housing lies at the foot of the Peña de Martos, a rock of 1,003 metres on the western side of the Sierra Sur de Jaén, a subrange of the Baetic System in the south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. Its economy has long rested on agriculture, above all the cultivation of the olive tree, and olive groves cover some 22,000 hectares, about 85 percent of the municipal area. The town has been nicknamed the Cradle of the Olive Grove.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city has been linked to the Roman settlement of Colonia Augusta Gemella. Around 250 CE a bishopric was established in what is now Martos under the name Tucci, on territory taken from the suppressed Diocese of Iliturgi, as a suffragan of the archdiocese of Sevilla. In 350 it lost territory to the new Diocese of Cástulo and regained it when that see was suppressed about 400. In 715 the see was itself suppressed to found the Diocese of Beaza. The recorded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> include Rogato, in office from about 675 to 688, and Teudisclo from about 693.</p><p>From the 8th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moors">Moors</a> ruled the city under various Islamic states for over 500 years. In 1225 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> and León received it from al-Bayyasi, in exchange for helping al-Bayyasi in his war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, and incorporated it into his kingdom. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it became a strategic place, and numerous fortifications were built to strengthen its defences.</p><p>At the end of the 19th century and the beginning of the 20th, Martos went through a period of economic and urban growth, reflected in its architecture, thanks largely to the industrial production of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a>. It became one of the most prosperous cities of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>. The population grew strongly in the first half of the 20th century, peaking at over 30,000 in the 1950s, and then fell as work was scarce and many people left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and the Spanish Levante.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Martos has a population of about 24,000, the fifth largest municipality of its province. It is also an industrial centre of inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>. The population rises unofficially in winter, when migrant workers come for the olive harvest.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Martos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Al-Bayyasi ruled Baeza and nearby towns as a Castilian ally and vassal, handing over castles such as <b>Martos</b> and Andújar in exchange for military help.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-baeza">Taifa of Baeza</a></li></ul></section>
